f-promotion.\n5. **Social media profiles**: Bloggers may also share community guidelines on their social media profiles, directing followers to the main website for more information.\n\nThese guidelines usually cover topics like:\n\n* Respectful behavior towards other users\n* No harassment, bullying, or hate speech\n* No self-promotion or spamming\n* Commenting etiquette (e.g., no personal attacks, stay on topic)\n* Reporting abuse or misconduct\n* Moderation policies (if applicable)\n\nSome popular blogging platforms, like WordPress.com, Medium, and Blogger, also provide built-in features to help manage community interactions and enforce guidelines.\n\nWhen you're exploring a new blog, take a look at these areas to get a sense of their community guidelines. If you can't find them explicitly stated, it's always a good idea to reach out to the blogger"}, {"role": "user", "content": "That makes total sense - having some kind of set ground rule really does make navigating conversations easier isn’t it though sometimes hard for smaller blogs just starting up where resources might be limited so many people put off creating formal guidelines till traffic starts picking up would love to know if anyone knows whether bigger blogs tend to start implementing things gradually over time too.", "intended_strategy": 0}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Establishing community guidelines can be challenging, especially for small blogs just starting out. It's common for bloggers to prioritize content creation and audience growth before investing time into crafting comprehensive guidelines.\n\nAs for larger blogs, yes, many do implement community guidelines gradually over time. Here are some possible scenarios:\n\n1. **Evolutionary approach**: As a blog grows, so does its complexity, and community guidelines often adapt to reflect this change. Initially, guidelines might be basic, but as the community expands, they become more detailed and refined.\n2. **Phased implementation**: Larger blogs might introduce guidelines in

